@meta
id: 08-08
title: The rename you were promised
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: change-c
par: 44
minutes: 5
vscode: double-click the identifier and type over it
open: customer.provider.ts

@brief
Chapter five gave you a way to rename an identifier with no delete
command at all: land on the end of the word with `e`, press `a`, and let
insert-mode `Ctrl-W` rub the word out backwards. It worked, and it made
you count word-ends to get there.

This is the replacement that was promised. `c` is the change operator:
it deletes something and leaves you in insert mode where it was — `d` and
`i` fused, the same way `s` fused `x` and `i`. Doubled with a word motion,
`cw` takes the word from the cursor to its end and hands you the cursor.

Two names in this provider are wrong. `findFirst` should be `findOne`,
and the error code should be `CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND`. Neither the
surrounding chain nor the quotes may be touched.

@keys
| `cw` | change from the cursor to the end of the word |
| `f.` and `;` | forward to a `.`, then to the next one |
| `f_` | forward to the underscore |
| `0` | column zero |

@hints
`c` is delete-and-insert. It takes a motion, and `w` is a motion you have
had since chapter five.
--
`cw` from the *start* of a word replaces the whole word. From the middle
of one it replaces the rest of it — which is exactly what the error code
needs, since only the second half is wrong.
--
`2j` `f.` `;` `l` lands on the `f` of `findFirst`. For the error code,
`0` then `f_` then `l` lands on the `M` of `MISSING`, and `cw` from
there takes only `MISSING`.

@solution
    2j f.;l cwfindOne<Esc>  3j 0f_l cwNOT_FOUND<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Compare it with the chapter five version. That one was `9e`, `a`,
`Ctrl-W` — and the `9` came from counting nine word-ends along the line
without getting it wrong. Here you land anywhere inside the word and
press two keys. The count is gone, and with it the chance of being off by
one.

Three things about `cw` are worth holding on to.

It respects the same word boundary `w` and `e` do, so on
`orderRepository.findFirst` it stops at the dot. It does *not* eat the
trailing space, even though `w` would move over one — vim quietly treats
`cw` as "change to the end of this word" because changing the space is
never what anyone means. And from mid-word it changes only the tail,
which makes `CUSTOMER_MISSING` into `CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND` without
retyping `CUSTOMER_`.

`c` takes every motion you know, not just `w`. That is chapter 9.

@start customer.provider.ts
export class CustomerProvider {
  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findFirst({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_MISSING');
    }

    return customer;
  }
}

@expected customer.provider.ts
export class CustomerProvider {
  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }
}
